    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fiber-optic undersea cables are the backbone of global communications and digital infrastructure. These cables, laid across thousands of miles of the ocean floor, transmit over 95 percent of global communications and facilitate over $10 trillion in financial transactions daily. As an island, Taiwan is almost entirely reliant on fifteen undersea cable systems for international communications. These cables are increasingly threatened by malicious “gray zone” activity by the People’s Republic of China (PRC). In 2023, two cables connecting the main Taiwanese island to its outlying Matsu Islands were severed by Chinese commercial vessels, resulting in a months-long communications outage.\r\n\r\nThis report presents decision-makers with innovative policy solutions to address Taiwan’s digital resiliency that are underexplored in existing literature. By enhancing resiliency, Taiwan raises the political and technical costs of damaging its digital infrastructure while narrowing the spectrum of harmful action in which the PRC may maintain plausible deniability.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Daud, H., Knightly, D., and Verville, F. (2024). Securing Taiwan's Undersea Cables. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gb537rr4074. https://doi.org/10.25740/gb537rr4074.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
